#!/usr/bin/python3
"""waydroid-pwd -- drive the host's PipeWire graph from inside Waydroid's Android.

See docs/56-pipewire-control.md for the investigation; the Android half is pw-app/.

WHY THIS IS NOT THE AUDIO WORK

docs/44 is about the data plane -- getting samples out of Android with fewer
shims and less latency -- and it concluded that nobody should write a native
PipeWire client for the guest, because pipewire-pulse already terminates the
PulseAudio protocol natively and a native client would buy a few milliseconds
against a HAL that spends 85 ms.

None of that transfers to graph *control*. The PulseAudio protocol carries
sinks, sources and sink-inputs -- a mixer -- and has no concept of a port or a
link, so there is nothing it already does well here: today the container has no
graph access at all, and there is no incumbent to compare against. Which is also
why this needs no overlay file, no vendor .so, no HAL change and no SELinux
work. It is a host daemon and an ordinary APK, and it is independent of
everything docs/44 proposes.

WHY A HOST DAEMON AND NOT A SOCKET IN THE CONTAINER

The tempting shortcut is to bind-mount /run/user/1000/pipewire-0 into the
container the way lxc.py already mounts the pulse socket at config_session:3.
Measured on this host on 2026-09-25, and rejected:

  * access.socket is commented out in /usr/share/pipewire/pipewire.conf (line
    191), so access.legacy applies and every client is
    pipewire.access=unrestricted. Anything that opens that socket gets the whole
    API -- including linking a sink's monitor port into itself, which is a
    recording tap on everything the desktop plays.
  * SELinux is Disabled *inside* the container (docs/50 measured it), so there
    is no per-app gate on a mounted path: the capability would be granted to
    every app at once, not to the patchbay.
  * PipeWire cannot tell container apps apart anyway. The Waydroid audio
    client's pipewire.sec.* props are pipewire-pulse's own -- sec.uid=1000,
    sec.pid=<pipewire-pulse>, sec.socket=pipewire-0 -- and the only props that
    say "waydroid" are application.* ones the client supplies about itself.

A token in the app's private directory is the opposite of all three: that
directory is mode 0700 owned by the app's uid, Android uids are real uids, and
the kernel enforces that regardless of SELinux being off inside the container.

WHY NO PIPEWIRE BINDINGS

`pw-dump -m` is already a delta protocol. Measured: it emits a sequence of
complete top-level JSON arrays -- `[` and `]` in column 0, everything nested
indented -- an initial full snapshot and then one array per change batch, with
removals arriving as {"id": N, "info": null}. So the entire graph mirror is a
subprocess and a line loop: no libpipewire, no ctypes, and no version coupling
to whatever PipeWire the host happens to run.

Acting on the graph is likewise CLI, and by *numeric id* throughout. pw-link's
name form is unparseable -- `Midi-Bridge:Midi Through: Port-0 (capture)` puts
the node:port separator inside the port name -- but it takes ids, and --linger
is its default so a link outlives the pw-link that made it.

DELIBERATELY NOT USED

  * binder, for the reason docs/50 settled: an ordinary app cannot reach an
    arbitrary binder name. ServiceManager.getService() is non-SDK on Android 13,
    the name would want an overlay service_contexts entry, untrusted_app would
    want a `find` allow rule, and docs/35's dontaudit'ed binder-transfer trap is
    underneath all of it.
  * pw-cli load-module as the way to add a node. pw-cli loads a module into its
    OWN pw_context and then exits, which is very likely why pw-loopback exists
    as a standalone binary at all. Persistent nodes here are supervised child
    processes instead -- which are also reversible by killing them, and cannot
    leak across a daemon restart.
  * PipeWire's SecurityContext (global 3 on this host, from
    module-protocol-native). It could mint a restricted socket for the
    container, but it has no CLI, it needs access.socket turned on host-wide --
    changing access for every client on the machine, not just Waydroid's -- and
    it still cannot distinguish apps inside the container.

PRIVILEGE

None. PipeWire is a user service, so this runs as a systemd --user unit owned by
the session's user. The one thing that does need root -- dropping the connection
profile into the Android app's private directory -- is waydroid-pwd-publish,
a separate unit, because there is no reason for the daemon holding a PipeWire
connection to be the process with the privilege.
"""

